d recommend it's the  scene in the Russian Ministry of Defense  the night after the bombing had stopped  but while NATO was poised to go into  Kosovo and I had gone to Moscow to  negotiate with the Russians the terms of  Russia's participation and the  implementation force that was about to  go in to Kosovo under NATO command and  before my very eyes I watched the high  command of the Russian military which is  to say the minister of defense the chief  of the general staff the top GRU a  military intelligence guy lie to me  repeatedly about the status of a Russian  unit that was making its way from Bosnia  towards Kosovo and we had every reason  to believe was about to go into Kosovo  without coordinating with with with NATO  and therefore setting up a confrontation  between Russia and NATO we were about to  snatch big-time defeat from the jaws of  victory now you know fortunately my  colleagues who would accompanied me to  Moscow were sitting outside the room  Rose conductin we're watching CNN so  they know exactly where the Russian  forces were and were able to send me in  notes from time to time but there was  there were a number of pieces of  evidence that night that the Russian  High Command was on its own and I don't  know if that's because Boris Yeltsin was  indisposed or what but i think it was  not only a disturbing episode for those  of us who witnessed it but i think it  obviously had an effect on the russian  civilian leadership and they put in some  additional measures after it was over it  ended well however and it ended well  because Boris Yeltsin wanted to go to  the g8 meeting in Cologne as a hero  which he did and he wanted to go with  all these issues that had been so  contentious including within his own  government resolved and we were  basically able to say here are the terms  that will  to resolve keep calm  strobe as a Russian ax stand a fellow  Russian 'used I'd like to ask you a  question about poverty sure there's  considerable dispute about how many  Russians have fallen into poverty since  1991 reasonably it's somewhere between  35 and seventy percent depending on how  you set the poverty level the government  today says is 55 dollars a month that  doesn't seem reasonable to me but  whatever the case an enormous number of  Russians have fallen into poverty and  Russian sociologists tell us that many  of them were middle class people like  ourselves which may be without precedent  in a previously at least semi modern  country during the 90s reading he saw  two soviet ologist statements of the  clinton administration some of us had  the impression that you were the only  person who worried about this question  of poverty in Russia there was his  famous statement you made that more  therapy and less shock was needed I  understand that you later regretted that  remark but I interpreted to mean that  you were thinking about this issue of  poverty and then you gave a speech  subsequently I think at Oxford on the  occasion of Isaiah Berlin or some event  that I read and there to you alluded to  it so the question is this um as you  watched poverty grow in Russia in the  90s what did you think I mean you see me  love russia or care about rush in a deep  way obviously what did you think about  this did you feel any i don't know what  did you think about it and looking back  since so many are still in poverty what  do you think about it in two respects in  a humane way humanitarian what me and  strategically in that this is the first  time so far as i know that a fully  nuclearized country has been  impoverished and nobody knows exactly  what that means but it can't mean  stability well Steve just on the yep  freed says this this was this is the  last question so this is the last answer  the I regretted having used the the  irresistible sound bite what Russia  needs is less shocked and more therapy  precisely because it was a cute  soundbite and of course drove the  Russian reformers who were trying to  apply modern discipline economics to  their country absolutely nuts didn't  earn me for that day any accolades in  the Department of Treasury either i  might add but it was the was a correct  analysis and i never have recanted the  point that i was that i was trying to  trying to make by the way i was not by  any stretch of the imagination the only  person in the US government who thought  about this concurred about this and i'll  tell you who else thought about it cared  about a lot and that was the russia hand  bill clinton and he didn't see anywhere  near as much of it as i did but i can  remember going there when we went to st.  Petersburg and down the mosque Moscow he  as he was driving around looking out the  limousine window he said you know a lot  of those people either one way or  another kind of giving us the finger he  said they don't like us very much  they're mad at us for some reason and he  said I think I know what it is and that  is they've whole lot of hardship and  heartache has come with this transition  and you know we got to do so I'm not  going to do the accent okay we gotta do  something about that and that's still I  think it's the it's the economy stupid  now the only point that I would put sort  of counter to the trend of our little  dialogue here where we're kind of in  sync is the good old days the good old  days weren't the good old days there was  an awful lot of poverty in the Soviet  Union that got disguised through phony  statistics just as there was an awful  lot of corruption and criminality which  like everything else was privatized in  the early 1990s but the main thing is  that the citizens of the old Soviet  Union were not real citizens and any  sense of the word that we would  recognize and they were profoundly  impoverished in the sense that they were  living under a dictatorship and Russians  now  the vote they can they can they can vote  in referendums they can vote for their  officials and over time let's hope that  they vote for officials who will make  that country as the Russians like to say  a normal modern country I think they've  got a very good shot at it but we  obviously have to help them in every way  we can thank you very much  she'll defend quite the evening and  you'll I get your autograph strobe  Talbot was deputy secretary of state  during the Clinton administration and  will assume the presidency of the  Brookings Institution later this year  the Russia hand is published by Random  House for more information or to read an  excerpt visit their web address at  random com
 

